    "body": "## Context\n\nA bug in the mobile app (now fixed) was silently stripping the `nip05` field from Kind 0 republishes when users edited their profile, which broke the `<handle>.divine.video → pubkey` binding on this server.\n\n- Root cause + reproduction: divinevideo/divine-mobile#4012\n- Detailed analysis: divinevideo/divine-mobile#4021\n- Fix (merged 2026-05-06): divinevideo/divine-mobile#4022\n\n## Affected user\n\n- Handle: `ike.divine.video`\n- Owner pubkey: `npub14mwc76ywjhu4xvfeafc83dwqltnnj9hndn3f44nwdjfas2tv7xlqy9gqkf`\n- Reporter: @ike-fyi\n- Zendesk ticket: `rabblelabs.zendesk.com/agent/tickets/1684`\n\nike is an early adopter who originally claimed `ike` and previously had a verified subdomain pointing at his profile. After he edited his profile in the mobile app, the bug republished a Kind 0 without `nip05`, the binding dropped on this server, and the slot was re-claimed by a legacy Vine-import account that holds ~1 video. The in-app `claimUsername` flow (`POST /api/username/claim`) now returns 409 (taken), so he cannot recover it himself.\n\n## Ask\n\nManually re-point `ike` → `npub14mwc76ywjhu4xvfeafc83dwqltnnj9hndn3f44nwdjfas2tv7xlqy9gqkf` on `names.divine.video`. If the conflict is a duplicate Vine-import claim rather than a fresh user registration, this likely needs to be coordinated with `divine-resurrection` to unwind the legacy assignment cleanly.\n\n## Other potentially-affected users\n\nAnyone who edited their profile on a vulnerable mobile build between the introduction of the bug and the merge of divinevideo/divine-mobile#4022 may have lost their verified handle the same way. Worth a one-time audit for other handles that no longer resolve to the pubkey that originally claimed them.",
